session:pr791Summary a agp:TranscriptSummary ;
    agp:summaryText "Issue #747 followed up on #389: SELECT/ASK queries with an undeclared prefix already returned HTTP 400, but the body was an opaque nom debug dump (ErrorKind::Verify plus a byte slice) that never named the offending prefix, unlike SPARQL Update's equivalent (turtle_parser-backed) message. The issue's stated scope was narrower than the actual fix required -- it assumed the remaining-input slice from #398's existing Failure was already reachable at the message-formatting layer, so only a formatting change was needed. Writing a throwaway debug-dump test first showed otherwise: SELECT/ASK/CONSTRUCT's triple-pattern loop (parse_group_graph_pattern_contents) silently discarded the hard nom::Err::Failure parse_prefixed_name raises for an undeclared prefix, replacing it with a generic Err::Error from a later fallback branch -- so by the time any formatter ran, the prefix name was already gone from the error value, regardless of how the message was built." ;
    agp:reasoningFor ghpull:791 ;
    agp:decisionPoint session:pr791Decision ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy session:pr791 ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo session:claudeSonnet5 .

session:pr791Decision a agp:Decision ;
    agp:summaryText "Two-layered fix: (1) stop the silent-discard sites in parse_group_graph_pattern_contents from replacing the Failure, verified via a new assert_undeclared_prefix_failure test helper that checks specifically for nom::Err::Failure rather than just is_err() (both outcomes still fail to parse, so the original tests could not distinguish a swallowed Failure from a genuine one); (2) add ParseFailureInfo (an owned, borrow-free summary of a nom::Err) and describe_parse_error in sparql_parser, which recognize the undeclared-prefix Failure specifically and build a message naming the prefix, suggesting a PREFIX declaration, and listing the prefixes that are already declared. ParseFailureInfo exists as a separate extraction step (not inlined at each call site) because nom::Err<Error<&'a str>> shares parse_query's 'a lifetime with its &mut ParserContext argument -- holding the raw nom::Err and reading ctx.prefixes in the same expression does not borrow-check, so the owned fields must be pulled out before ctx is read again." ;
    agp:alternative "Considered a new public field on ParserContext (e.g. last_failed_prefix) set as a side effect during parsing -- rejected: ParserContext is constructed via struct literal at roughly 70 call sites across the workspace, so adding a field would require touching all of them for a value only the error path needs. Also considered a thread-local side channel -- rejected given this codebase's existing caution around thread-locals for parser state (see PR #361's BaseGuard/BnodeMemoGuard precedent, which required careful save/restore specifically because of subquery re-entrancy): a parse failure's remaining-input slice is already sufficient to recover the prefix name without needing any new channel at all." .
